Hi Bob,

Thanks for looking into this, As I wrote: " I have removed the part relating to the covariates for clarity. ".This in order to focus on the part that is more difficult to model without polluting the view. In the final model  predictors are included.

regarding to:

  for (i in 1:Ntests) {
     Cutpoints[i][1] ~ normal(CutpointsMean[1] , CutpointsSigma[1]);
     Cutpoints[i][2] ~ normal(CutpointsMean[2] , CutpointsSigma[2]);
     Cutpoints[i][3] ~ normal(CutpointsMean[3] , CutpointsSigma[3]);
  }

Efficiency aside, is the above a correct way of modelling a hierarchical ordinal response in Stan? I'm new to Stan and I'm not quite sure on how the internals of the ordered vector work. Said that, the results I get seem sensible.

In order to enforce the ordering constraints more explicitly, I have also created another model
where the first cutpoint is modelled as a normal distribution while the other two cutpoints are modelled as two half normal distributions that represent a relative positive offset respect to the previous cutpoint.
The final cutpoints are computed by adding the first cutpoint and the other two cutpoints offsets as appropriate:

 parameters {
    real          Cutpoints1[Ntests]; // cutpoint 1 group i
    real<lower=0> Cutpoints2[Ntests]; // offset cutpoint 2 group i
    real<lower=0> Cutpoints3[Ntests]; // offset cutpoint 3 group i

    real          CutpointsMean1;     // global cutpoint 1
    real<lower=0> CutpointsDMean2;    // global cutpoint 2 offset
    real<lower=0> CutpointsDMean3;    // global cutpoint 3 offset

    real<lower=0> CutpointsSigma[3];  // global cutponts sigma values

    ...
 }

 model {

    CutpointsSigma   ~ exponential(1);
    CutpointsMean1   ~ normal(1, 1);   // these priors are problem specific
    CutpointsDMean2  ~ normal(1.5, 1.5);
    CutpointsDMean3  ~ normal(2, 2);

    for (i in 1:Ntests) {

      Cutpoints1[i] ~ normal(CutpointsMean1 , CutpointsSigma[1]); // cutpoint 1 group i
      Cutpoints2[i] ~ normal(CutpointsDMean2, CutpointsSigma[2]); // delta cutpoint 2
      Cutpoints3[i] ~ normal(CutpointsDMean3, CutpointsSigma[3]); // delta cutpoint 3
    }

  for (i in 1:N) {

      vector[3]  cuts;
      vector[3]  ocuts;

      cuts[1]    <-  Cutpoints1[tests[i]]; // from normal
      cuts[2]    <-  Cutpoints2[tests[i]]; // from normal > 0 (half normal)
      cuts[3]    <-  Cutpoints3[tests[i]]; // from normal > 0 (half normal)

      // recover cutpoints from offsets
      ocuts[1] <- cuts[1];                            // can take any value
      ocuts[2] <- cuts[1] + cuts[2];                  // ocuts[2] > ocuts[1]
      ocuts[3] <- cuts[1] + cuts[2] + cuts[3];        // ocuts[3] > ocuts[2] > ocuts[1]

      y[i] ~ ordered_logistic("predictors go here", ocuts);

  }

Both the above models  produce very similar and sensible looking results, however as I'm new to Stan I may have missed something?

Thanks,
Antonio

Dear all,

I’m trying to fit an ‘optimal observer’ model for a human learning task (psychology) in which each of my data samples is the sum of two normal processes. Specifically in the task, there are 2 players on each trial working together to produce one summed outcome, by playing 1 of 2 games (stimuli). In total there are 3 agents (out of which 2 are playing on each round). Each agent has for each game a pay-off drawn from a normal distribution. The pay offs from the two players are averaged together. Overall there are 60 rounds. I am trying to build a model that tells me the most likely parameters for each agent for each stimulus, given data observed so far (for example, what is the most likely values for an agent after I have observed 2 rounds, what about after 20 rounds?).

The model is:

data{
  int mtr;             // trials (=rounds) per block
  int nstim;           // number of stimuli per block
  int nplayers;         // number of agents per block -1
  int stims[mtr];  // stim on each trial
  int players[mtr,2]; // players on each trial
  vector[mtr] outs;  // magnitude outcome on each trial - is rounded to integer
}

transformed data{
  vector[mtr] outs2;
  outs2 <-outs*2; // this is to deal with the fact that ‘outs’ is the average of the two players

}

parameters{
  real<lower=-6,upper=6> parasMt[mtr,nplayers+1,nstim]; // trial specific estimates  /1=bls
  real<lower=0,upper=1>  parasSDt[mtr,nplayers+1,nstim]; //very narrow range for now to see whether it runs, should change later?
  vector<lower=-1,upper=1>[mtr] roundError; // rounding error is max. 0.5, but outs2 is 2*outs, so also have to double the error
}

transformed parameters{
  vector[mtr] outsR;
  outsR<-outs2-roundError;
}

model{
  int cstim;
  int cplay1;
  int cplay2;

  // Priors
  roundError ~ normal(0,10);
  for (it in 1:mtr){
    for (ip in 1:3){
      parasMt[it,ip,:]  ~ normal(0,10);
      parasSDt[it,ip,:] ~ normal(0,10);
    }}

  // Model
    for (t in 1:mtr){
      for (i in 1:t){
        cstim    <- stims[i];
        cplay1   <- players[i,1];
        cplay2   <- players[i,2];
        outsR[i] ~ normal(parasMt[t,cplay1,cstim]+parasMt[t,cplay2,cstim],sqrt(parasSDt[t,cplay1,cstim]^2+parasSDt[t,cplay2,cstim]^2));
      }
    }
}

1) When I try to sample this, it does not converge (even with 2000 samples each for burnin and sampling). However, when I make a variant of the model that only tells me the belief in the last round, it does converge (with 500,500 samples). It seems to me like the model above which models a parameter estimate for each trial should be running in the same way? The model with only the belief for the last round is:
parameters{
  real<lower=-6,upper=6> parasMt[nplayers+1,nstim]; // trial specific estimates  /1=bls
  real<lower=0,upper=1>  parasSDt[nplayers+1,nstim]; //very narrow range for now to see whether it runs, should cahnge later?
  vector<lower=-1,upper=1>[mtr] roundError; // rounding error is max. 0.5, but outs2 is 2*outs, so also have to double the error
}

transformed parameters{
  vector[mtr] outsR;
  outsR<-outs2-roundError;
}

model{
  int cstim;
  int cplay1;
  int cplay2;

  // Model
    for (t in 1:mtr){
        cstim    <- stims[t];
        cplay1   <- players[t,1];
        cplay2   <- players[t,2];
        outsR[t] ~ normal(parasMt[cplay1,cstim]+parasMt[cplay2,cstim],sqrt(parasSDt[cplay1,cstim]^2+parasSDt[cplay2,cstim]^2));
    }
}

2) There is a warning when compiling the model and I’m sorry I don’t understand from reading the manual whether this may be causing the problem: “compile: Warning (non-fatal): Left-hand side of sampling statement (~) may contain a non-linear transform of a parameter or local variable. If so, you need to call increment_log_prob() with the log absolute determinant of the Jacobian of the transform. Left-hand-side of sampling statement:  outsR[i] ~ normal(…)”
I’m not sure whether this is maybe related to the way I’m trying to add up two randomly drawn values? I also tried coding this as
outsR[t] ~ outsR[i] ~  normal(parasMt[t,cplay1,cstim],parasSDt[t,cplay1,cstim]) + normal(parasMt[t,cplay2,cstim],parasSDt[t,cplay2,cstim]);
but this didn’t even compile

3) It’s only on trial 6 that each combination of stimulus and player has occurred at least once (see temp.data.R) and obviously not each parameter affect each trial; I am not sure whether that might be causing problem? (but i’m giving each variable a prior which I thought would prevent this being a problem?

I would greatly appreciate any help with this.
Jacquie

Hi Bob,

Thanks a lot for the pointers, I have rewritten the model using the Ordered Inverse Transform as follows:

parameters {
  vector[K-1]   Cutpoints[Ntests]; // no longer ordered
  vector[K-1]   CutpointsMean;
  real<lower=0> CutpointsSigma[K-1];

  ...
}

model {

  CutpointsSigma ~ exponential(1);

  CutpointsMean  ~ normal(0, 10);

 // The vectorized version didn't work for some reason, I will double check later on .

  for (i in 1:Ntests) {
    Cutpoints[i][1] ~ normal(CutpointsMean[1] , CutpointsSigma[1]);
    Cutpoints[i][2] ~ normal(CutpointsMean[2] , CutpointsSigma[2]);
    Cutpoints[i][3] ~ normal(CutpointsMean[3] , CutpointsSigma[3]);
  }

  for (i in 1:N)
  {
    vector[3]  cuts;

    // map to ordered sequence by Ordered Inverse Transform
    cuts    <- Cutpoints[tests[i]];
    cuts[2] <- cuts[1] + exp(cuts[2]);
    cuts[3] <- cuts[2] + exp(cuts[3]);

    y[i] ~ ordered_logistic("predictors go here", cuts);
  }
}

'

As you can see, the cutpoints are no longer declared as ordered variables.The distribution of the ordinal response is computed after the model fit by applying the ordered inverse transform to the posterior of the unordered cutpoints.

Would this work or there is still something missing?:)

Thanks,Antonio

