Hello everyone,

I have written a relatively straightforward linear model in Stan to capture a data-generating process in which users give their perceptions of the placement of a series of items on a scale. Some users are precise in their placements of the items and others are error-prone. Some items are also more difficult to place than others. I want to capture heterogeneity in individual-specific error using a multiplicative term (as done in the literature): item-specific error x individual-specific error. I have set up a model that works well with both simulated and real data without the term to capture heterogeneity in item-placement error. However, when I build on this simpler model by adding in the multiplicative term, the n_eff plummets into the single digits, and the model takes forever to fit. Does anyone have any thoughts on what might be going wrong here?

Thanks in advance!

data {

  int n_items;
  int n_respondents;
  int n_obs;

  int i[n_obs];  // respondent i
  int j[n_obs];  // item j
  real z[n_obs]; // item response z_ij

}

parameters {

  vector[n_respondents] alpha; // Individual-specific shift
  vector[n_respondents] beta; // Individual-specific stretch

  vector[n_items] zeta_raw; // Latent 'true' position of item
  real zeta_mu;
  real<lower = 0> zeta_sigma;

  real<lower = 0> tau_j[n_items]; // Item-specific variance for placement (some items more difficult to place than others)
  real<lower = 0> tau_k; // Item-specific variance for a unique item in the data

  real<lower = 0> tau_i[n_respondents]; // Individual-specific variance for item placement
  real<lower = 0> tau_i_sigma;

  vector[n_respondents] theta; // Latent position of individual-specific unique item

}

transformed parameters {
  vector[n_items] zeta;

  zeta[1] = -1; // For identification
  zeta[2] = 1;
  for(m in 3:n_items)
    zeta[m] = zeta_raw[m];

}

model {

  // Priors
  alpha ~ normal(0, 10);
  beta ~ normal(0, 10);

  zeta_mu ~ normal(0, 10);
  zeta_sigma ~ uniform(0, 100);
  zeta_raw ~ normal(zeta_mu, zeta_sigma);

  theta ~ normal(0, 1); // For identification

  tau_j ~ normal(0, 5);
  tau_k ~ normal(0, 5);

  tau_i_sigma ~ normal(0, 5);
  tau_i ~ normal(1, tau_i_sigma);

  // Likelihood
  for(m in 1:n_obs) {
    if(j[m] == (n_items + 1))
